import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../Ui/Versus

## File-based routing, as SvelteKit has it: this site's own pages are routed this way.
meta = {
	title: "Routing",
	description: "File-based routing in rocx: +page.rocx, +layout.rocx, +server.roc and +error.rocx under routes/, params typed by their directories, middleware named by the file it guards, a typed path for every page, a typed function for every endpoint, and their OpenAPI document.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "files", label: "The files" },
		{ id: "page", label: "A page" },
		{ id: "written", label: "What rocx writes for it" },
		{ id: "layouts", label: "Layouts" },
		{ id: "params", label: "Params, and typed links" },
		{ id: "endpoints", label: "Endpoints: +server.roc" },
		{ id: "openapi", label: "Their OpenAPI document" },
		{ id: "middleware", label: "Middleware: what runs, in what order" },
		{ id: "router", label: "The router, and the app" },
		{ id: "error", label: "Where no page is" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Routing"
				title="A page is a file"
			>
				Under an app's <code>routes/</code>, a <code>+page.rocx</code> is a page at the path its
				directory names, each <code>+layout.rocx</code> above it wraps it, a <code>+server.roc</code>
				answers HTTP methods there, and <code>+error.rocx</code> shows where nothing is, as in SvelteKit.
				A file names the middleware it runs behind. rocx writes the router, a path for every page and a
				function for every endpoint, so a link to a page that isn't there, or a call to an endpoint that
				isn't, doesn't compile.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="files">The files</Heading>
			<p>These are this site's: every page you can open here is one of them.</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_tree.file} source={Sources.route_tree.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog/anything</code>, given <code>{"params.slug"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>+layout.rocx</code></td><td>around every page in its directory and below</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+server.roc</code></td><td>the endpoints at <code>/blog</code>: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code>, <code>delete!</code>, and <code>events!</code>, a stream; <code>respond!</code>, any other method, answered as it likes</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/.well-known/…</code></td><td>routed, as RFC 8615 has it; another directory starting with a dot isn't</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+error.rocx</code></td><td>what a path nothing answers shows, with the status 404, and a page that failed (404 when its data isn't there, else 500)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>middleware = …</code></td><td>in a page or a <code>+server</code>: what it runs behind</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="page">A page</Heading>
			<p>
				A route file is bare: its imports, then its definitions. <code>view</code> renders the
				page; <code>meta</code>, if it has one, is given to its layouts (here, the title, the description and
				what the sidebar on the right lists).
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page.file} source={Sources.route_page.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>view</code> is given a record:</strong> <code>request</code> (its path and headers,
					cookies among them), <code>params</code> (<code>{"{}"}</code> on a page without), and
					<code>context</code>, what <a href="#middleware">middleware</a> handed on (this site's default
					hands on the facts its build measured). Take what you need, and <code>..</code> for the rest.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>view!</code></strong>, a page that does something as it renders (a query, an endpoint
					asked there and then): the <a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a> page is one.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Imports are relative to the file</strong>, as everywhere in rocx: this one is two directories
					down, so the site's parts are <code>../../Ui</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Components of its own</strong>, as any <code>.rocx</code> file may have
					(<code>{"Board := [].{ view! = … }"}</code>), go before its definitions or after them, not between:
					rocx writes them as types beside the page's module, where their <code>view</code> isn't the page's
					(<a href={"${Paths.components}#title"}>an example</a>). <code>{"<Board />"}</code> calls the file's own.
					They see the file's definitions as the page does: a helper defined beside <code>view!</code> is
					theirs to call too, unless one names something of its own the same.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="written">What rocx writes for it</Heading>
			<p>
				Roc names a module after its file, and <code>+page</code> isn't a name, so rocx writes each route file
				as a module of its own under <code>Generated/</code>, named after its path
				(<code>ShowcasePage</code>). Its imports are moved to where it is, and the module's line goes on the
				blank line after them (or, with none, at the start of the first definition's line), so line
				<var>N</var> is still line <var>N</var> of the route file, and the compiler's reports point into it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page_generated.file} source={Sources.route_page_generated.rocx} />

			<Heading id="layouts">Layouts</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+layout.rocx</code> is around every page in its directory and below, the outermost first. Its
				<code>view</code> is given the request, the context, the page's <code>meta</code>, and the page (or the
				next layout) as <code>children</code>. This site has one, the document around every page:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_layout.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_layout.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="params">Params, and typed links</Heading>
			<p>
				A directory in brackets is a param: <code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code> is the page at any
				<code>/blog/…</code>, given <code>{"params : { slug : Str }"}</code>. A path with a fixed part is
				tried before one with a param there, so <code>/blog/new</code> can be a page of its own. A
				directory may name a file, with dots between letters or digits: <code>routes/sitemap.xml/+server.roc</code>
				answers <code>/sitemap.xml</code>, and its names drop the dot (<code>Api.get_sitemap_xml</code>). This page,
				from the app the tests build:
			</p>
			<Code title="test/e2e/routes/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx" source={Sources.route_fixture_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				rocx also writes <code>Paths</code>: a path for every page, a function of its params if it has any.
				Link to a page through it, and a link to one that isn't there, or with the wrong params, doesn't
				compile, and the compiler says so at your line (<code>{"Paths.blog_slug({ slg: \"x\" })"}</code>:
				<em>Maybe slg should be slug?</em>).
			</p>
			<Code title={"What rocx writes for that app: ${Sources.route_fixture_paths.file}"} source={Sources.route_fixture_paths.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>This site's sidebar is a list of them:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_nav.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_nav.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="endpoints">Endpoints: +server.roc</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+server.roc</code>, plain Roc (<code>+server.rocx</code> if it has markup), answers HTTP
				methods at its path: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code> and
				<code>delete!</code>, each given the request, the params, the context and (with a body) the body, read
				when it says it is JSON (<code>application/json</code>) or a form. A body of any other type, an
				upload, isn't read: the handler takes <code>{"{}"}</code> and reads <code>request.body</code>, the bytes as
				they came. A handler answers a result, sent as JSON, or a refusal, sent as its status (or an answer
				of its own: <code>Err(Answer(Server.redirect(…)))</code>). A form a browser posts without the
				runtime gets neither: it goes back to the page it came from, with a flash that page reads
				(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#forms"}>forms</a>). A <code>respond!</code>, given the
				request, the params and the context, answers every method the file doesn't name with a
				<code>Server.Response</code> of its own: a page mounted from elsewhere, a JSON-RPC endpoint. <code>events!</code> answers a GET
				that asks for events with a stream of them, sent as they are made. Beside a page, a GET a browser
				sends is the page's, and every other request the endpoints'. Each handler is annotated, and rocx
				writes <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> from those annotations, the app's contract: a function for
				each, its method, URL and types, which the app calls and any module may import (a job, an actor);
				<code>Generated/Handlers.roc</code> binds each to its handler, for the server.
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#endpoints"}>Talking to the server</a> has this site's,
				live, and <a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>a stream</a> too. This one answers a form, and sets a
				cookie:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.members_join_server.file} source={Sources.members_join_server.rocx} />

			<Heading id="openapi">Their OpenAPI document</Heading>
			<p>
				An app can serve its endpoints' OpenAPI document (3.2): each path and its params, each method and
				what the comment above its handler says, and the JSON Schemas of what it is sent and what it
				answers. A schema is the handler's type, as the compiler has it, so one nobody wrote down is there
				too: this site's <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code> has no annotation, and its document says it
				answers <code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>. The endpoints a page reads are in
				it too, since a page's data is an endpoint as any other.
			</p>
			<h3>Turning it on</h3>
			<p>It is off unless the app says so, with <code>openapi</code> in its <code>App.program</code>:</p>
			<Code title="main.roc" source={"program = App.program({\n\tcontext: |started| { started },\n\tmiddleware: Middlewares.default,\n\topenapi: Page(Swagger),\n})"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>openapi:</code></th><th>The app serves</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Off</code>, or no <code>openapi</code></td><td>neither</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Document</code></td><td><code>GET /openapi.json</code>: the document, for a client generator</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Page(Swagger)</code>, <code>Page(Scalar)</code></td><td>that, and <code>GET /openapi.html</code>: the document in Swagger UI, or in Scalar</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Behind the app's middleware</strong>, as every answer is: a guard that keeps a page
					from strangers keeps these from them too.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The page loads its viewer from jsDelivr</strong>, pinned to one version and checked
					against its hash, and tells it not to call home: no validator for Swagger UI; no telemetry, fonts,
					developer tools or agent for Scalar. Bundled instead, a viewer would add 2 to 5 MB to every app's
					server.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An app that writes its own <code>program</code></strong> puts
					<code>OpenApi.middleware(Page(Swagger), Api.openapi)</code> at the end of its middleware chain,
					as <code>App.program</code> does, or asks <code>OpenApi.respond</code> before its routes, as this
					site's does (<a href="#middleware">below</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This site serves both: <a href="/openapi.html">its API, in Swagger UI</a>, and
				<a href="/openapi.json">the document</a>.
			</p>
			<h3>Who makes it, and when</h3>
			<p>
				Neither platform: rocx writes Roc that asks for it, the compiler works it out, and the server only
				sends it.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What runs</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rocx build</code></td>
							<td>rocx (<code>rocx-cli/Routes.roc</code>), as it writes the router</td>
							<td>
								Writes Roc, and knows no types. <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> gets <code>openapi</code>: each
								path, its params and its methods, with their comments, and for each endpoint a call that asks
								<code>OpenApi</code> for the schemas of its body and its answer; and <code>tags</code>, every name
								in the app's code that could be a tag. <code>Generated/Routes.roc</code> gets <code>/openapi.json</code>.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>roc build</code>, the server's</td>
							<td>the Roc compiler</td>
							<td>
								Infers each endpoint's types; writes, for each, a parser for the format <code>OpenApi.Schema</code>,
								as it writes one for <code>Json</code> to read the endpoint's body; and, <code>openapi</code> being a
								constant, works it out as it compiles. The server's binary holds the document, as text.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>GET /openapi.json</code></td>
							<td>the server (native)</td>
							<td>Sends that text, if the app turned it on, behind its middleware, like every answer.</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The browser's build (wasm) has none of it: its copy of <code>Api</code> is the same contract (each
				endpoint's method, URL and types), and no document. What rocx writes for this site, in part:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_api.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<h3>A parser that writes</h3>
			<p>
				Roc has no reflection: no code can ask what a type is. But the parser the compiler writes for a type
				isn't JSON's: it is one for any format, which it calls, shape by shape, to read each part (a record,
				its next field, a string, a number), and it hands a record's format the names of its fields.
				<code>OpenApi.Schema</code> is a format that reads nothing: each part it is asked to read, it writes
				the schema of, as <code>Json</code> writes that part, and gives back a value nothing looks at.
				<code>OpenApi.body</code> asks the compiler for the parser of what the endpoint is sent, for
				<code>Schema</code>, and runs it:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_format.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_format.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				So for <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code>'s answer, <code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>,
				the parser and <code>Schema</code> go:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The parser calls</th><th><code>Schema</code> writes</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_start</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, given the names <code>bytes</code>, <code>shouted</code>, <code>word</code>: it hands back <code>bytes</code></td><td><code>{"\"bytes\":"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_u64</code>, as <code>bytes</code> is a <code>U64</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code> and <code>parse_str</code>, for <code>shouted</code>, then <code>word</code></td><td><code>{",\"shouted\":{\"type\":\"string\"}"}</code>, and <code>word</code>'s</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, with no name left: it says <code>Done</code></td><td><code>{"},\"required\":[\"bytes\",\"shouted\",\"word\"]}"}</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A tag union's parser is the one part that doesn't say what it has: it asks the format for a tag's
				name, then reads that tag's payloads. So rocx gives the document <code>tags</code>, every name in the
				app's code that could be one (its own, and its packages' and platforms'), and <code>Schema</code>
				answers each in turn: a name that isn't one of the union's, the parser turns down; one that is, it
				reads the payloads of, and <code>Schema</code> writes their schemas. <code>{"[Red, Rgb(U8, U8, U8)]"}</code>
				is a string, <code>"Red"</code>, or an object, <code>{"{ \"Rgb\": [r, g, b] }"}</code>, as <code>Json</code>
				writes them.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A GET with events</strong> (<code>events!</code>) answers <code>text/event-stream</code>
					too: each event's data is JSON, with its schema, and an <code>end</code> event ends the stream.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A refusal</strong> is every method's default answer: its status, and why, as text.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A type of your own</strong> (<code>{"Email := Str.{ parser_for : _ }"}</code>) is described as
					<code>Json</code> reads it. Write its own parser for any format (<code>encoding.parse_str</code>), not
					for JSON alone, and the document can describe it too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="middleware">Middleware: what runs, in what order</Heading>
			<p>
				Middleware is code that runs around an answer. It is given the request, the context so far and what
				comes next (<code>next!</code>), and may answer itself (a guard: a redirect, a 401), pass the request
				on with more context (who is asking), or change the answer that comes back (a header). A guard, a
				session, headers, a log: one idea, and <code>a.then(b)</code> is <code>a</code>, then <code>b</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.middleware_type.file}, in part"} source={Sources.middleware_type.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Two places name middleware, and nothing else runs any. The app's default runs around every request,
				in its <code>respond!</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_main.file} source={Sources.route_main.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A route file names its own, <code>middleware = …</code>, which runs after the default, around that
				file's page or endpoints, and hands on the context they are given (an endpoint's handler reads it
				as <code>context</code>: <code>{"get! = |{ context, .. }| …"}</code>). This site's:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.site_middlewares.file} source={Sources.site_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.members_page.file}, in part"} source={Sources.members_page.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.members_server.file} source={Sources.members_server.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				So a request to <code>/members</code> goes through the app's default, then
				<code>Middlewares.member</code>, then the page (for a browser) or <code>get!</code>; the answer goes
				back out through them, gathering the default's headers. The page reads <code>GET /members</code> as
				it renders, and the server answers that the same way, behind the same middleware.
				<code>rocx routes</code> lists every route, the file that answers it, and what it runs behind:
			</p>
			<Code title="A terminal" source={Sources.route_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The context is typed.</strong> <code>member</code> is a
					<code>{"Middleware({ facts : Str }, { facts : Str, member : Str })"}</code>, so the members' endpoint
					reads <code>context.member</code>; one that read it without that middleware wouldn't compile. A
					layout reads only what every page's context has (<code>{"{ facts, .. }"}</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Each file names its own.</strong> A page's middleware doesn't guard the endpoints beside it,
					or the pages below: <code>members/+server.roc</code> names <code>Middlewares.member</code> too.
					What guards a route is in its file, and <code>rocx routes</code> shows them all, together.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Try it, with <code>just dev</code>.</strong> <a href={Paths.members}>/members</a> sends you
					to join; join, and it greets you by the name you gave. A copy of this site as files has no server
					to ask, so these pages aren't in it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="router">The router, and the app</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Generated/Routes.roc</code> has one function: the answer to a request. It takes the path
				apart and tries each, the fixed before the params; there, the page for a browser's GET, in its
				layouts, or the endpoint for the method, or a 405; and the error page where nothing is:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_routes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_routes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The app's server answers every request with it, behind its default middleware (<a href="#middleware">above</a>),
				the GETs it makes as a page renders too. A file that names middleware has its page and its
				endpoints wrapped in <code>Middleware.run!</code>, given what the middleware passes on:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_members.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_members.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="error">Where no page is</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>routes/+error.rocx</code> renders what a path nothing answers shows, in the layouts at the root,
				for a browser, and the status is 404. It is also what a page shows when it fails and nothing in it
				catches that: a read its endpoint refused with a 4xx keeps that status (<code>NotFound</code>, the
				page's 404), and anything else is a 500. This site's lists the pages of the part of the docs the path is in (try <a href="/nowhere">/nowhere</a>, or <a href="/platform/nowhere">/platform/nowhere</a>):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_error.file} source={Sources.route_error.rocx} />

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<Callout kind="note" title="Navigation is a page load">
				<p>
					A link loads the next page from the server, as on any page of plain HTML, and the browser
					hydrates it from the start. Navigating in the browser, without a page load, is next.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A static build writes the pages without params</strong> (<code>Paths.all</code>); one with
					params would need a list of the ones to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One error page</strong>, the app's, at the root of <code>routes/</code>; and layouts aren't
					given the params.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No rest params</strong> (<code>[...path]</code>) or groups (<code>(marketing)</code>) yet,
					and no middleware for a whole directory: each file names its own.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The OpenAPI document</strong> calls every field required, describes a type that holds
					itself (a tree) only so deep, and has no <code>$ref</code>s; it is titled after the app's
					directory. A tag union's tags come from the names in the code until Roc's parser says them, as it
					says a record's fields.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
